WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.704
Compare total cost of ownership rather than one GPU or API price.

00:00:04.704 --> 00:00:09.307
Local cost includes hardware, replacement, power, cooling, storage, support, and operator time.

00:00:09.307 --> 00:00:13.911
Cloud GPU cost includes running and idle hours, disks, snapshots, and transfer.

00:00:13.911 --> 00:00:16.597
Hosted APIs add request or token charges.

00:00:16.597 --> 00:00:20.050
Hybrid integration adds gateways, monitoring, testing, and policy maintenance.

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:23.502
Allocate these costs to the workloads that create them.

00:00:23.502 --> 00:00:25.804
Measure end-to-end performance from the user.

00:00:25.804 --> 00:00:30.024
Local generation may avoid network delay but use a smaller model.

00:00:30.024 --> 00:00:35.395
Cloud generation may be faster after arrival yet slower overall because of routing, upload,

00:00:35.395 --> 00:00:36.930
queueing, or cold start.

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:42.301
Track time to first output, completion, throughput, error rate, and queue time by route.

00:00:42.301 --> 00:00:46.137
Include prompt size and data preparation so comparisons remain meaningful.

00:00:46.137 --> 00:00:47.672
Reliability requires understanding dependencies.

00:00:47.672 --> 00:00:51.508
Local service depends on owned hardware, power, cooling, and maintenance.

00:00:51.508 --> 00:00:56.496
Cloud service depends on network, provider region, account quota, credentials, and rate limits.

00:00:56.496 --> 00:01:01.483
A failover path must be healthy, compatible, affordable, and authorized for the request.

00:01:01.483 --> 00:01:06.087
Test failover and recovery rather than inferring them from an architecture diagram.

00:01:06.087 --> 00:01:08.772
Monitor real demand before scaling either side.

00:01:08.772 --> 00:01:13.376
Idle local hardware and unused cloud reservations waste different kinds of money.

00:01:13.376 --> 00:01:18.747
Automatic cloud fallback can create runaway spending during a local outage, so use budgets,

00:01:18.747 --> 00:01:21.049
rate limits, queue caps, and alerts.

00:01:21.049 --> 00:01:26.420
Optimize the combined service for workload value, not for maximizing use of either environment.
